  "content": {
    "text": "When you buy through links on our articles, Future and its syndication partners may earn a commission.\nBelow is a weekly earnings calendar of the most important upcoming quarterly reports scheduled to be released by publicly traded companies. However, we have also provided expanded previews and recaps for select companies that are reporting earnings throughout the week.\nPlease check back often. This earnings calendar is updated regularly.\n(Editor's note: Earnings dates in tables are tentative. However, companies featured in \"Earnings Spotlights\" have officially announced their earnings dates.)\nEarnings calendar highlights\nMonday (6/22)\nThere are no noteworthy earnings reports scheduled for release on Monday, June 22.\nTuesday (6/23)\nEarnings calendar preview: FedEx\nFedEx (FDX) is one of a handful of notable names on a relatively light earnings calendar, with the logistics giant slated to disclose its fiscal fourth-quarter earnings after Tuesday's close.\nTrack all markets on TradingView\nDeutsche Bank analyst Richa Harnain expects \"a complicated earnings release\" for FedEx, given \"fiscal year calendarization and the expected upcoming restatements associated with the company's recent Freight spin-out.\"\nLooking for more timely stock market news to help gauge the health of your portfolio? Sign up for Closing Bell, our free newsletter that's delivered straight to your inbox at the close of each trading day.\nFedEx completed its spinoff of its less-than-truckload segment in mid-May, though financials from the now separate FedEx Freight Holding Company (FDXF) will be included in FDX's fiscal Q4 results.\nHarnain expects FedEx to report earnings per share of $6.41 for its fiscal Q4, which is 5.6% higher than the year prior and more than 8% above the consensus estimate of $5.92. Analysts expect revenue to arrive at $24.0 billion, up 8% year over year.\nWednesday (6/24)\nEarnings calendar preview: Micron Technology\nMicron Technology (MU) is one of the hottest stocks of the year so far as a supply and demand imbalance boosts prices for the memory chips it produces.\nYear to date, the semiconductor stock has surged nearly fourfold — and Wall Street expects explosive growth for its fiscal third quarter results, too, which are due out after Wednesday's close.",
